According to Section 3 of the text passage [1] (source: legal_mutual_nda_commonpaper.txt), the four exceptions under which the Receiving Party's confidentiality obligations do not apply are:

(a) information that is or becomes publicly available through no fault of the Receiving Party;

(b) information that the Receiving Party rightfully knew or possessed prior to receipt from the Disclosing Party without confidentiality restrictions;

(c) information that the Receiving Party rightfully obtained from a third party without confidentiality restrictions;

(d) information that the Receiving Party independently developed without using or referencing the Confidential Information.

These exceptions are stated in Section 3 of the MNDA.

According to Section 3 of [2] (source: legal_mutual_nda_commonpaper.txt), the Receiving Party's obligations in this MNDA do not apply to information that it can demonstrate:

(a) is or becomes publicly available through no fault of the Receiving Party;

(b) it rightfully knew or possessed prior to receipt from the Disclosing Party without confidentiality restrictions;

(c) it rightfully obtained from a third party without confidentiality restrictions;

(d) it independently developed without using or referencing the Confidential Information.

These are the four exceptions under which the Receiving Party's confidentiality obligations do not apply.
